  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain showers and isolated thunderstorms return to \nthe region tonight through Sunday.\n\nRegional radar shows a swath of precipitation from the Mid-West to \nwestern NY this afternoon. An abundant amount of dry air across the \nregion will result in continued dry weather through most of the \nafternoon. A few sprinkles can't be ruled out across far western NY \nlate this afternoon. \n\nSurface high pressure will move across New England this afternoon, \nbefore moving off the east coast tonight. Surface low pressure is \nexpected to develop across the Mid-West today, then move east along \na surface trough while approaching the region tonight. Strong low to \nmid-level isentropic lift will move across the eastern Great Lakes \nregion through tonight. Deep moisture with PWATS over 1.5\" (97th \npercentile) will enter western NY. Showers will expand in coverage \nfrom southwest to northeast across western NY tonight. Dry weather \nwill continue across north-central NY through this evening, before \nshowers enter late tonight into Sunday morning. Pockets of heavy \nrain are possible across the western Southern Tier late tonight as \nthe column saturates. Models show little to no MUCAPE overnight, \nhowever horizontal wind shear in the mid-levels and deep moisture \nmay result in a rumble of thunder. \n\nThe main region of isentropic lift will move east of western NY late \ntonight, while moving into central/north-central NY. This will bring \na break in steady rain across western NY, however drizzle may \npersist especially across the higher terrain. Deep moisture with \nPWATS over 1.4\" (90th percentile) will enter north-central NY. Light \nto moderate showers will spread across the Tug Hill region, with the \nheaviest rain staying south of the Saint Lawrence River through \nSunday morning. Surface high pressure across Ontario/Quebec will \nbuild into the region Sunday. The surface trough will push south \nacross western NY, while bending northward into the Adirondack \nregion. Showers will taper off from north to south. A shortwave \ntrough with available moisture will support additional showers \nacross the western Southern Tier to north-central NY through Sunday \nafternoon. The building high will support dry weather across the \nregion Sunday night through Monday. \n\nBasin average rainfall amounts will range from less than 0.25\" \nacross the Saint Lawrence River to the Niagara Frontier to 0.50\"-\n0.75\" near the NY/PA line and southern Tug Hill region. Rainfall\namounts have trended down from previous forecasts.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below normal temperatures will continue into next \nweek. \n\nCanadian high pressure will reside north of the border through a \nmajority of next week. A cool, northeast flow will maintain below \nnormal temperatures across the region. The coolest days will be \nMonday through Wednesday with highs in the low to mid 60s, upper 50s \nacross the higher terrain. Low temperatures will fall to the 40s, \nhowever the upper 30s are possible across the Black River valley. \n\nThere remains uncertainty with the northward extent of precipitation \nand sky cover associated with low pressure across the Appalachians \nMonday night through Tuesday, and this may impact temperatures early \nin the week. Mean QPF and MSLP suggest that the high will build far \nenough south for showers to be possible across the western Southern \nTier, however in scenarios where the high is weaker, measurable QPF \ncould move as far north as I-90. Currently the forecast has low \nchances (20-30%) across far western NY.",
